<h2 style="font-size:1.6rem; color:var(--primary-color); margin-top:2rem; margin-bottom:1rem; font-family:var(--font-heading);">Who is a Consumer?</h2>Under the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, a consumer is any person who buys goods or avails services for consideration for personal use.<p style="margin-bottom:1.5rem; color:var(--text-muted);"></p><hr style="border:0; border-top:1px solid var(--border-color); margin:2.5rem 0;" /><p style="margin-bottom:1.5rem; color:var(--text-muted);"></p><h2 style="font-size:1.6rem; color:var(--primary-color); margin-top:2rem; margin-bottom:1rem; font-family:var(--font-heading);">Common Grounds for Consumer Complaints</h2>- <strong>Defective Goods:</strong> Manufacturing defects, adulterated items, or non-conformance to warranties.
- <strong>Deficiency of Service:</strong> Negligence or substandard service by banks, insurance companies, builders, airlines, or hospitals.
- <strong>Unfair Trade Practices:</strong> Charging above MRP, misleading advertisements, or refusal to issue tax invoices.<p style="margin-bottom:1.5rem; color:var(--text-muted);"></p><hr style="border:0; border-top:1px solid var(--border-color); margin:2.5rem 0;" /><p style="margin-bottom:1.5rem; color:var(--text-muted);"></p><h2 style="font-size:1.6rem; color:var(--primary-color); margin-top:2rem; margin-bottom:1rem; font-family:var(--font-heading);">Procedure for Instituting a Consumer Complaint</h2>1. <strong>Formal Legal Notice:</strong> Issue a 15-day statutory notice demanding replacement, refund, or rectification.
2. <strong>District Consumer Commission (DCDRC):</strong> File a complaint before the District Commission for claims up to ₹50 Lakhs.
3. <strong>Documentation:</strong> Attach purchase invoices, warranty cards, correspondence, and technical reports.
4. <strong>Reliefs Claimable:</strong> Replacement of product, full monetary refund, and compensation for mental agony and litigation costs.
